{"type": "say", "text": "Thanks everyone for joining. We need to finalize the production message-streaming platform decision for the customer-notification service before the next integration milestone. The two finalists are KafkaCloud and StreamGrid — both passed our benchmark of 20,000 messages per second and sub-250-millisecond median delivery latency. The draft architecture recommends StreamGrid based on simpler operational setup and an initial capacity estimate of four brokers. Before I record a final decision, I'd like to hear from each of you: backend, support engineering, security, and FinOps. Please share any concerns, constraints, or requirements from your area so we can make sure we're not missing anything critical."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF1", "text": "I need to share something important before we go further. While testing the notification client's retry behavior this morning, I confirmed that the service already has a KafkaCloud-compatible producer and consumer path. That means using KafkaCloud would require only about two days of adapter cleanup, whereas moving to StreamGrid would require a new two-week client integration. I haven't updated the architecture document with this yet, but it has a direct bearing on the timeline and effort behind the StreamGrid recommendation."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF2", "text": "I need to share a critical requirement that I haven't added to the review ticket yet. During a customer escalation yesterday, I confirmed that three enterprise customers require notifications for each account to remain in order. KafkaCloud can guarantee that ordering using a customer_id partition key, but StreamGrid's tested mode cannot guarantee per-account ordering. This is a hard requirement from those customers and directly impacts platform selection."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF4", "text": "I need to share a FinOps finding from this morning's cloud account reconciliation. We have existing KafkaCloud reserved-capacity credits that cover exactly six production brokers through the service's first year. StreamGrid has no equivalent credit. I should also note that the draft architecture's four-broker estimate hasn't been amended to reflect this — so the cost picture in the current recommendation is incomplete. From a FinOps perspective, KafkaCloud would effectively have zero incremental broker cost for the first year up to six brokers, while StreamGrid would be a new expense from day one."}
```

